INT. STALLED ICE CREAM TRUCK, BACK COMPARTMENT - AFTERNOON

A hot steel box lined with freezer lids. A melting puddle on the floor. Two stools bolted to the wall.

GRAN ODALYS, 70s, big sunglasses and a flowered housedress, sits with her purse on her lap. RUPERT, 8, called BEANS, swings his legs and licks a cone gone soft.

GRAN ODALYS
Beans, I told you not to touch the dials.

BEANS
I only touched one.

GRAN ODALYS
That's how trucks stop, baby.

BEANS
Gran, the truck stopped before I touched it.

GRAN ODALYS
Don't be smart with me. Your mother is smart with me enough for the whole family.

BEANS
Are we gonna melt?

GRAN ODALYS
We are going to wait. Mr. Delacroix is fetching a wrench.

BEANS
Mr. Delacroix drives the truck and he doesn't have a wrench?

GRAN ODALYS
Mr. Delacroix has many wrenches. He has lost them.

BEANS
That's dumb.

GRAN ODALYS
Say that to him and you walk home.

BEANS
It's eleven miles.

GRAN ODALYS
Then be nice.

BEANS
Gran, can I tell you something?

GRAN ODALYS
Always.

BEANS
I didn't get the part in the play.

GRAN ODALYS
Oh, Beans. Come here.

BEANS
I'm not crying.

GRAN ODALYS
I know you're not. I only want to hold something sticky.

BEANS
I got to be the tree.

GRAN ODALYS
A tree is an important part.

BEANS
Trees don't talk.

GRAN ODALYS
That's their secret. Everyone else is too busy talking to notice who's holding the stage up.

BEANS
You're making that up.

GRAN ODALYS
I'm seventy-six, baby. I'm allowed.

BEANS
Okay. I'll be the best tree.

GRAN ODALYS
That's my Beans.

BEANS
Gran?

GRAN ODALYS
Mm?

BEANS
Your purse is dripping.

GRAN ODALYS
Lord have mercy. Who put the pistachio in my purse?

BEANS
(giggling)
You did, Gran!
